<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Emirate of Crete</h1><p class="st-meta">824 CE – 961 CE (approx.) · capital Chandax</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Muslim emirate founded by Andalusian exiles that held Crete for over a century and made it a base for raiding the Byzantine Aegean.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After a failed revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Cordoba</a> in 818, thousands of Andalusians were expelled from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a>. Many reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, which they held for some years before being forced out. Led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-hafs-umar-al-iqritishi">Abu Hafs Umar</a>, they landed on Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> in the 820s, conquered the island and founded a fortified capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraklion">Chandax</a>, on the site of modern Heraklion.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> ruled as a hereditary dynasty, nominally recognising the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>, and Arabic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> spread among part of the population. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> grew rich from raiding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> coasts, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> and commerce with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a>, and minted its own coins. It repelled several Byzantine expeditions, including those of 843, 911 and 949.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 960 the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-ii-phokas">Nikephoros Phokas</a> landed a great army and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraklion">Chandax</a> after a winter siege in March 961. The last emir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-aziz-ibn-shuayb">Abd al-Aziz ibn Shu'ayb</a>, was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> returned to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a> and was re-Christianised.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/emirate-of-crete.jpg" alt="Emirate of Crete" width="320" height="216"><figcaption>Image: MWahaiibii · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emirate_of_Crete_Map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>824 CE – 961 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chandax</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abu Hafs Umar, Abd al-Aziz ibn Shu'ayb</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Medieval Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Andalusian Emirate of Crete, Iqritish</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=892&amp;focus=emirate-of-crete" aria-label="Emirate of Crete on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5310 901 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Emirate of Crete lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5630 1157L6134 1224M5630 1157L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6134 1224h0M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5630 1157h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5630 1157h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=892&amp;focus=emirate-of-crete">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

